# CODEOWNERS — the part that actually enforces ownership.
#
# .canon-owners warns the author locally; THIS routes review on the server and,
# with branch protection enabled, prevents a merge without the owner's approval.
# You want both: the local one catches the honest mistake early, this one is the
# gate that cannot be bypassed with --no-verify.
#
# TO MAKE THIS REAL, you must also protect the default branch:
#   Settings → Branches (or Rules → Rulesets) → protect `main`:
#     ✔ Require a pull request before merging
#     ✔ Require review from Code Owners
#     ✔ Do not allow bypassing the above settings
#
# Without branch protection CODEOWNERS only requests reviews — it blocks nothing.
#
# PLAN CAVEAT: on GitHub, branch protection / rulesets for PRIVATE repositories
# requires a paid plan (Team or Enterprise). On a free private repo you get
# CODEOWNERS review requests but no enforcement. Verify against your own plan
# before relying on this; if you are on free, .canon-owners plus a social norm is
# what you actually have.
#
# Syntax: <path pattern>  <@user|@org/team|email>
# Last matching rule wins — the OPPOSITE of .canon-owners (first match wins).
# Keep the two files in the same order to avoid surprises.

# Default: no owner, anyone may merge.
# (Deliberately absent — a catch-all owner means every session note needs review.)

# Leadership-owned direction
/Vision/                     @your-org/leadership

# The router is injected into every agent's context every session
/Vision/Agent Router.md       @your-org/leadership @your-org/staff-eng

# Governance config itself must be governed, or it governs nothing
/.canon-owners                @your-org/leadership
/.github/CODEOWNERS           @your-org/leadership

# The scan suppression files decide what secrets can slip through
/.canon-scan-allow            @your-org/security
/.canon-scan-exclude          @your-org/security
